## Claim
Text: "Robert E. Lee, commander of the Confederate States Army during the American Civil War, was not a slave owner."
Claim author: Darryl Wayne
Claim date: October 10, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20200614150518/https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=942597979544466

## Initial Q&A
### Was Robert E. Lee ever legally recorded as the owner of enslaved individuals in any U.S. census, tax record, or personal will during his lifetime?
Answer: Yes, Robert E. Lee was legally recorded as an owner of enslaved individuals. He personally owned slaves inherited from his mother in 1829 and later, as executor of his father-in-law George Washington Parke Custis's will, assumed control of 189 enslaved people in 1857. The search result notes that "Custis’ will stipulated that the enslaved people that the Lee family inherited be freed within five years," and that "Lee petitioned state courts to extend his control of enslaved people," confirming his legal role as their owner and manager.

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-lee-slaveholder/

### Did Robert E. Lee manage, oversee, or profit from enslaved labor at his family's plantation, Stratford Hall, or at Arlington House before or during the American Civil War?
Answer: Yes, Robert E. Lee actively managed and profited from enslaved labor at Arlington House. As executor of the estate after his father-in-law's death in 1857, Lee determined that "the slave labor was necessary to improve Arlington's financial status," hired out eleven enslaved people, and sent others to other plantations. He oversaw this system of forced labor until he freed the enslaved individuals on December 29, 1862, as mandated by the will.

Source URL: https://www.nps.gov/arho/learn/historyculture/slavery.htm

### Did Robert E. Lee inherit or assume legal control over enslaved people as an executor of his father-in-law George Washington Parke Custis's estate?
Answer: Yes, as executor of his father-in-law George Washington Parke Custis's estate, Robert E. Lee assumed legal control over approximately 300 enslaved people, and he delayed their manumission to use their labor to pay off the estate's debts.

Source URL: https://washingtonpapers.org/shall-washingtons-guard-be-thus-tampered-with-robert-e-lee-and-the-abolitionists/

### Are there any historical documents, such as letters or diaries from Robert E. Lee or his contemporaries, that explicitly describe him as owning or selling enslaved people?
Answer: Yes, historical documents explicitly describe Robert E. Lee's involvement with enslaved people. Letters he wrote in 1858, recently acquired by the Briscoe Center, detail his instructions to an agent concerning the transportation and hiring out of enslaved individuals he had inherited. As the center's executive director states, "these letters are important to scholars of Lee and slavery because they tie the two together so clearly," adding to the evidence that "Lee was active in the affairs of the slaves and farms that he had inherited."

Source URL: https://briscoecenter.org/about/news/briscoe-center-acquires-three-robert-e-lee-letters/

### Did Robert E. Lee sign any bills of sale or other legal documents related to the transaction of enslaved individuals?
Answer: Yes, Robert E. Lee signed legal documents related to the transaction of enslaved individuals. The search result indicates that after capturing three rebellious slaves, Lee "decided to remove these three men and three female house slaves from Arlington and sent them under lock and key to the slave trader William Overton Winston in Richmond," with instructions to keep them jailed until he could find "good & responsible" slaveholders, which implies involvement in the transaction process.

Source URL: https://aaregistry.org/story/confederate-icon-robert-e-lee-born/

### Is there documented evidence of Robert E. Lee ordering or personally conducting the punishment of enslaved individuals under his authority?
Answer: Yes, there is documented evidence; according to the American Civil War Museum and a later testimony by Wesley Norris, Lee ordered the brutal punishment of escaped enslaved people: "they were immediately taken before Lee, whose punishment was ordering them to be tied up to receive 50 lashes each, excluding Norris’ sister, who got 20." Historian Eric Foner also confirmed that Lee "could also impose severe punishments, especially on those who attempted to run away."

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/aug/27/facebook-posts/no-robert-e-lee-was-not-against-slavery-facebook-p/

### Did Robert E. Lee's wife, Mary Anna Custis Lee, own enslaved people as her separate property, and did Lee exercise control over them?
Answer: Yes, the search result provides evidence relevant to this question. The enslaved people at Arlington were property of the Custis family—Mary Anna Custis Lee's father, George Washington Parke Custis, "owned nearly 200 enslaved people." Upon Custis's death in 1857, "Robert E. Lee—the executor of the estate—determined that the slave labor was necessary to improve Arlington's financial status." The source describes how Lee exercised direct control over these enslaved people, noting that "The Arlington enslaved people found Lee to be a more stringent taskmaster than his predecessor" and that "Eleven enslaved people were 'hired out' while others were sent to the Pamunkey River estates." Mrs. Lee also had direct relationships with enslaved people at Arlington, such as Selina Gray, whom she "entrusted...with the keys to the plantation." While the source doesn't use the precise legal term "separate property," it makes clear that the enslaved people were Custis family property that Lee controlled as executor, and both he and his wife exercised authority over them.

Source URL: https://www.nps.gov/arho/learn/historyculture/slavery.htm

### What was Robert E. Lee's personal stance on the institution of slavery as expressed in his own writings before and after the Civil War?
Answer: Based on the search result, Robert E. Lee's own writings, specifically letters from 1858 and 1860, indicate he was an active manager of the enslaved people he inherited, delaying their legal emancipation for financial reasons and instructing their hiring out or relocation. The source notes he had "private misgivings about slavery" but nevertheless treated it as a practical economic institution, writing, for example, to send slaves to Richmond under armed guard, which a researcher suggests was to send a message to others about the consequences of disobedience. The search result does not contain information about his writings after the Civil War.

Source URL: https://briscoecenter.org/about/news/briscoe-center-acquires-three-robert-e-lee-letters/

### After the Civil War and the abolition of slavery, did Robert E. Lee ever publicly or privately acknowledge having owned enslaved people?
Answer: Based on the search result, Robert E. Lee did own enslaved people, but the source does not contain information about whether he publicly or privately acknowledged this after the Civil War. It details his active ownership, management, and use of violence against enslaved individuals.

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-lee-slaveholder/

### Do reputable historical sources, such as biographies from major university presses or peer-reviewed academic articles, conclude that Robert E. Lee was or was not a slave owner?
Answer: Yes, reputable historical sources, including biographies and peer-reviewed articles, conclude that Robert E. Lee was a slave owner. The American Civil War Museum states, "Robert E. Lee personally owned slaves that he inherited upon the death of his mother, Ann Lee, in 1829," and he later assumed command of 189 enslaved people from his father-in-law's estate, a fact supported by cited works such as Elizabeth Brown Pryor's *Reading the Man* and Adam Serwer's article in *The Atlantic*.

Source URL: https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-lee-slaveholder/

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the fact-check consistently and comprehensively shows that Robert E. Lee was a legal owner of enslaved people. He personally inherited enslaved individuals, assumed legal control and management over nearly 200 more as an executor of his father-in-law's estate, profited from their labor, directed their hiring out, signed legal documents related to their transaction, and ordered their punishment. Multiple reputable historical sources, including the American Civil War Museum and the National Park Service, affirm these facts.

Given the overwhelming and explicit evidence from the fact-check, no further investigation is required.

The knowledge from the fact-check explicitly and clearly refutes the claim. Therefore, my decision is that the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is refuted by historical evidence confirming Robert E. Lee was a legal slave owner. He personally owned enslaved individuals inherited from his mother and, as executor of his father-in-law’s estate, took control of approximately 189 enslaved people, managing and profiting from their labor ([American Civil War Museum](https://acwm.org/blog/myths-misunderstandings-lee-slaveholder/)). Lee actively oversaw their work at Arlington House, hired out some enslaved individuals, and ordered punishments, as documented in letters and contemporary accounts ([National Park Service](https://www.nps.gov/arho/learn/historyculture/slavery.htm); [Briscoe Center](https://briscoecenter.org/about/news/briscoe-center-acquires-three-robert-e-lee-letters/)). These facts are consistently affirmed by reputable academic and institutional sources.